      "headline": "Sr. Site Reliability Engineer",
      "about": "In my years in systems, I've come up with four rules:\n1. Don't be a monkey. Monkey's can be trained to perform the same task repeatedly. Your job is to look beyond that and let the computer be the monkey.\n2. There's no such thing as a 15 minute task. Context switch alone will cost you more than this. If someone is asking you to do something, it's already more than 15 minutes. Always assume that any give task is at least half a day's work.\n3. There's no such thing as a one-off. Anything you're doing now will need to be done again. From there, see Rule 1.\n4. Every temporary solution becomes permanent. Once a group has found a workable solution to a problem, they move on. If the 'workable solution' is 'assign to a person', see Rule 1. Try to solve the problem as best as you can the first time, because  you should expect that solution to be around for a very long time.",
